    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1217">

                <pb facs="1083" n="769" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg769"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 367.116/228</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State
                    </hi> to 
                        <hi rendition="#italic">G. T. W. Patrick</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-09">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 9, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your November 6.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1217-f1"/> Personal telegram to James L.
                    Barton, of Boston, dated November 7 and transmitted by American Ambassador at
                    Constantinople reads as follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>American Ambassador with hearty cooperation of Turkish officials has
                        situation completely in hand. Missionaries and their work fully safeguarded.
                        Everything proceeding as though normal conditions prevailed. You may safely
                        reassure all friends.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peet</hi>
                        
                        <lb/>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
